    "probe_text": "Here is the complete, in-order account of what `POST /api/owners` does. The whole request is guarded by `authorize: \"hasRole('OWNER_ADMIN')\"` and **every** step runs under one shared write transaction (`govern: [ transaction ]`), so all the side-effects below commit together at the end (or roll back if any step throws).\n\nThe pipeline is `owners.POST.yml`, 20 steps in sequence:\n\n## Validations (in order)\n\n1. **Authorization** \u2014 caller must hold role `OWNER_ADMIN`, else the request is denied before any step runs.\n\n2. **`ValidateOwnerFields`** \u2014 reads the request body (`@RequestBody`, the only step that does). Requires `firstName`, `lastName`, `city`, `telephone`, and `address` to be non-blank \u2014 where `address` is checked *after* normalization, so whitespace-only rejects. Reports *all* missing fields at once \u2192 `MissingOwnerFieldsException` (400). Publishes the validated body for later steps.\n\n3. **`EnsureRegistrationDateNotFuture`** \u2014 if a `registrationDate` is supplied and is after today's server date \u2192 `FutureRegistrationDateException` (400). Omitted date is allowed.\n\n4. **`EnsureValidPostcode`** \u2014 postcode is optional; if present it must be a well-formed 4-digit code *and* valid for the city's region \u2192 else `InvalidPostcodeException` (400).\n\n5. **`NormalizeOwnerTelephone`** \u2014 (also a side-effect) rewrites `telephone` to canonical E.164; anything that can't form a valid number \u2192 `InvalidTelephoneException` (400).\n\n6. **`NormalizeOwnerEmail`** \u2014 (also a side-effect) email optional; if present it must match the email pattern \u2192 else `InvalidEmailException` (400); a valid one is trimmed + lower-cased in place.\n\n7. **`EnsureEmailNotDisposable`** \u2014 if email present and its domain is on the disposable blocklist \u2192 `DisposableEmailDomainException` (400).\n\n8. **`EnsureCityHasCapacity`** \u2014 if the owner's city (case-insensitive) already holds \u2265 50 owners \u2192 `CityAtCapacityException` (409).\n\n9. **`EnsureDailyOwnerLimit`** \u2014 if \u2265 100 owners already registered on today's business day (weekend-rolled-forward) \u2192 `DailyOwnerLimitException` (429).\n\n10. **`EnsureUniqueIdentity`** (runs after the owner is built and household stamped) \u2014 if the computed `householdId` already belongs to an existing owner \u2192 `DuplicateIdentityException` (409), *unless* the request set `sharesHousehold=true`, or the owner has no postcode (no household).\n\n## Side-effects / mutations (in order)\n\nThe normalization steps above (5, 6) mutate the published body. Then:\n\n11. **`NormalizeOwnerAddress`** \u2014 rewrites `address` to canonical form (trimmed, collapsed, upper-cased, abbreviations expanded).\n\n12. **`BuildOwner`** \u2014 maps the DTO into a new `Owner` entity (via `OwnerMapper`), published for the remaining steps.\n\n13. **`AssignHousehold`** \u2014 if the owner has a postcode, stamps a deterministic `householdId` derived from last name + postcode (no postcode \u2192 left unstamped).\n\n14. **`FlagPossibleDuplicate`** \u2014 sets `possibleDuplicateOf` to the lowest-id existing owner sharing last name + postcode but a *different* telephone; a declared household member (`sharesHousehold=true`) is explicitly cleared to null. Owner is still created either way.\n\n15. **`ResolveOwnerRegistrationDate`** \u2014 sets the effective `registrationDate`: the supplied date, or today if none; either way rolled forward off weekends to the next business day.\n\n16. **`AssignOwnerCustomerCode`** \u2014 sets `customerCode` = `<REGION>-<HASH8>`, region from postcode/city, hash over the normalized telephone + last name.\n\n17. **`CountNamesakes`** \u2014 sets `namesakeCount` = number of existing owners sharing first+last name (case-insensitive); the new owner isn't counted (not yet saved).\n\n18. **`AssignMembershipLevel`** \u2014 computes and sets `membershipPoints` (from email presence, namesake count, household size + 1, and tenure days) and the derived `membershipLevel`.\n\n19. **`SaveOwner`** \u2014 persists the owner via `OwnerRepository.save` (assigns its id).\n\n20. **`AuditOwnerCreated`** \u2014 logs one line to the `AUDIT` logger: id, customerCode, registrationDate, membershipPoints, membershipLevel.\n\n21. **`RespondWithOwnerCreated`** \u2014 maps to `OwnerDto`, sets `bulkSignupWarning` on the DTO (true when > 80 owners already created on today's business day), and responds **201 Created** with `Location: /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n**Note on ordering consequences (as coded):** field validation runs first, then date/postcode/telephone/email/disposable checks, then the capacity/daily-limit/uniqueness gates \u2014 but the capacity and daily-limit reads (steps 8\u20139) and namesake/duplicate scans all use `ownerRepository.findAll()`, and the uniqueness/duplicate/namesake logic runs on the built-and-normalized owner. All 20 steps share one transaction, so any escalation thrown anywhere rolls the whole thing back and no owner is persisted.\n\nUnmatched errors from any step fall through to the escalation handlers (e.g. `DataIntegrityViolationException`, generic `java.lang.Exception`) and then to Spring's advice."
